[2.0, "o", "\u001b[2m15:54\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mThe clock is at 22:29 sim with one departure left, so I'll make a last inbox check and write the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m15:54\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mclosing note, then release the seat once I know nothing is open.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.436, "o", "\u001b[2m15:55\u001b[0m  \u001b[33m\u25b6 inbox\u001b[0m\u001b[2m()\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.442, "o", "\u001b[2m15:55\u001b[0m    \u001b[36m\u21b3 inbox\u001b[0m \u001b[2mnull\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[2.0, "o", "\u001b[2m16:01\u001b[0m  \u001b[32m\u270e note\u001b[0m \u001b[32mClosing note, 22:30 sim. Three weather cells over the Canaries, Madeira and the Valencia coast slotted our morning wave and the delays rolled through the fleet as late aircraft all day, with 3,135 bags short-shipped behind the rushed turns. I answered all 159 decisions and defaulted none: took slots under 20 minutes and sent REA on the rest (every REA got an improvement), announced every delay so passengers heard first, rushed every bag, held fares on eight cuts and matched three where loads were soft and the cut shallow, and never cancelled a flight or lost a crew. Jet2 finishes top of the lobby at 139.6 with zero cancellations against Aer Lingus 138.8, Flybe 138.1 and Air France 138.0, all autopilot carriers, and against the 126 to 139 band the autopilot produced across the field; the bag bill of 47m is the price of the weather rather than of any choice, though next time I would retime the tightest regulated turns pre-emptively to leave fewer bags on the ramp, and watch"]
[0.0, "o", " the LS254 and LS335 tails earlier for the four-hour delays that built up unnoticed.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.451, "o", "\u001b[2m16:02\u001b[0m    \u001b[36m\u21b3 note\u001b[0m \u001b[2mnoted\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[2.0, "o", "\u001b[2m16:05\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mInbox is clear and the closing note is on the tape. Releasing the seat.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.013, "o", "\u001b[2m16:05\u001b[0m  \u001b[33m\u25b6 release_seat\u001b[0m\u001b[2m()\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.591, "o", "\u001b[2m16:05\u001b[0m    \u001b[36m\u21b3 release_seat\u001b[0m \u001b[2mreleased\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[2.0, "o", "\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mThe day is done and the seat is released. Replay: http://127.0.0.1:8095/replay/c073fc0e1c88\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m**What happened.** Three weather cells hit Jet2's core leisure routes: the Canaries and Madeira from\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mmid-morning and the Valencia coast from late morning. The regulations slotted the morning wave, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mthose delays rolled through the fleet as late aircraft for the rest of the day. The worst were LS335\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mNCL-LPA and LS254 MAH-EMA at close to four hours. Behind every rushed turn came a short-shipped bag\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mcall.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m**What I did.** I answered all 159 decisions and defaulted none.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m- **Slots:** took anything under 20 minutes, sent REA on the rest. Every REA came back with an\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mimprovement.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m- **Ops:** announced every d"]
[0.0, "o", "elay so passengers heard before reaching the airport.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m- **Ground:** rushed every bag set, never held one for tomorrow.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m- **Pricing:** held fares on eight competitor cuts and matched three, where the cut was shallow and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mour loads were soft (LPA-MAN, FAO-GLA, NCL-ACE).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m- **Crew:** no crew ever timed out, so no reserves were needed and nothing was cancelled.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m**Result.** Jet2 finished top of the lobby with zero cancellations.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m| Carrier | Score | Cancelled |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m|---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m| LS (Claude) | 139.6 | 0 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m| EI (autopilot) | 138.8 | 0 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m| BE (autopilot) | 138.1 | 1 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m| AF (autopilot) | 138.0 | 0 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mThe autopilot carriers of similar size landed between"]
[0.0, "o", " 126 and 139. The gap between us and them was\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mthe bag bill, which the weather set rather than any decision.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97m**What I would do differently.** Retime the tightest regulated turns pre-emptively to leave fewer\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mbags on the ramp, and watch the tails behind LS254 and LS335 earlier, since those four-hour delays\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[97mbuilt up without any decision asking me about them until they were already large.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.327, "o", "\u001b[2m16:16\u001b[0m  \u001b[1m\u2014 run ended: success \u00b7 465 turns \u00b7 $14.85\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.502, "x", "0"]
